{#- Three words, and no branch named anywhere a person can see:

     Save      already happened, on the page you were editing
     Preview   build next.thauma.one so you can look at it
     Publish   build thauma.one — the site changes

   The page is mostly a LIST because publishing carries every change that has
   been saved, not only the last one you made. A button with nothing behind it
   is a button you press without knowing what it does. -#}

<p class="rolegate" id="pNotConfigured" hidden></p>

<div class="adm-publish" id="pRoot" hidden>

  <div class="p-state" id="pState"></div>

  {#- Above the button, and repeated inside the dialog. A migration is the one
     thing here that is not fixed by the deploy itself: the code goes out
     expecting a table the live database has not got, and the site breaks
     after publishing rather than before.

     This panel asks the DATABASE what it has, rather than inferring it from
     the files in the release. Those are different questions, and the gap
     between them is exactly the bug this page is meant to catch: on
     2026-08-17 the live database was two migrations behind and nothing said
     so, because no migration file had changed recently. -#}
  <div class="p-migrations" id="pMigrations" hidden></div>

  <div class="p-cols">
    <section class="p-col">
      <h3 data-i18n="pub.changes">Changes</h3>
      <div class="p-commits" id="pCommits"></div>
    </section>
    <section class="p-col">
      <h3 data-i18n="pub.files">Files</h3>
      <div class="p-files" id="pFiles"></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  {#- MANUAL ACTIONS, deliberately behind a disclosure.

     The buttons in the bar do the normal thing and are disabled when the
     normal thing is not needed. These do it ANYWAY. That matters because
     "nothing is waiting" is a claim assembled from GitHub's answer about the
     last successful run, and if that claim is ever wrong the page would
     otherwise offer no way to act on it — which is precisely the state this
     site was in when the live branch had code that had never been deployed.

     Folded away rather than hidden: findable when something is wrong,
     not sitting next to the ordinary buttons inviting a misclick. -#}
  <details class="p-manual" id="pManual">
    <summary data-i18n="pub.manualTitle">Something looks out of step</summary>
    <p class="p-manual-why" data-i18n="pub.manualWhy">
      Use these when the page says everything is up to date but the site
      disagrees. They do the same work as the buttons above, without waiting
      to be told it is needed.
    </p>
    <div class="p-manual-acts">
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="pForcePreview"
              data-i18n="pub.forcePreview">Rebuild the preview site</button>
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="pForcePublish"
              data-i18n="pub.forcePublish">Rebuild the live site</button>
    </div>
    <p class="p-manual-note" id="pManualNote"></p>
  </details>

  {#- MOVING DATA, which a deploy has never done.
     Publish and Preview carry code and schema. Rows have always had to be
     moved by hand, which meant a terminal, which meant they were not moved.
     This is the same job with a button on it.

     Hidden entirely where it is not offered — staging and production hold no
     credential for reaching another database, so the section simply is not
     there rather than being present and refusing. -#}
  <section class="p-sync" id="pSync" hidden>
    <h2 data-i18n="sync.title">Move data between sites</h2>
    <p class="p-sync-why" data-i18n="sync.why">
      Publishing carries the words and the code. It has never carried the
      records — accounts, mailing lists, subscribers, videos. This does.
    </p>

    <div class="p-sync-counts" id="pSyncCounts"></div>

    {#- SAID BEFORE THE BUTTONS, not in the dialog after them. Whichever way
       this runs, the other side's rows are gone — there is no merge, because
       there is no honest answer to "edited in both places". -#}
    <p class="p-sync-warn" data-i18n="sync.replaces">
      <b>Each direction replaces the other side.</b> Whatever is on the
      receiving end is gone, not merged. Decide where the work happened and
      send it that way, once.
    </p>

    <div class="p-sync-acts">
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="pSyncPush" data-i18n="sync.push">
        Send this site's data to staging</button>
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="pSyncPull" data-i18n="sync.pull">
        Bring staging's data here</button>
    </div>
    <p class="p-sync-note" id="pSyncNote"></p>
  </section>

  <p class="p-sync-off" id="pSyncOff" hidden></p>

</div>

<div class="ms-savebar" id="pBar" role="status" hidden>
  <span class="ms-dirty-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span id="pBarCount"></span>
  <span class="ms-savebar-note" id="pBarNote"></span>
  <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="pRefresh" data-i18n="pub.refresh">Refresh</button>
  <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="pPreview" data-i18n="pub.preview">Preview</button>
  <button type="button" class="solid-btn" id="pPublish" data-i18n="pub.publish">Publish</button>
</div>
